I just want to make sure there isn't any misunderstanding about the request. My concern isn't about when a new Miniconda version will be certified, but rather about the naming convention used for the certified Python base images.

Previously, the images were named after the packaged Python version (e.g. `3.13`, `3.14`), which provided a stable image name. This allowed Renovate to automatically recommend upgrades when new Python versions became available.

With the current naming convention based on the Miniconda release, Renovate can no longer recommend upgrades across Python versions. As a result, teams are no longer receiving automated upgrade recommendations and may continue using older Python base images unless they manually discover and switch to a different image.

I would suggest setting up a quick call with @Harpal, as he can explain the Renovate behaviour in more detail if helpful. It is important that we get the naming convention right so Renovate continues to work as intended, as it did previously.

Thanks.
